        {
            "id": "32732988",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "00:11, 22 August 2024",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(11,861 bytes)",
            "change": 384,
            "summary": "(Replaced bad references for wikiHaus content team)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "sourcing",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Mark the wall studs with a [[Find a Stud|stud finder]]. Arrange the device so it’s completely flush with the wall, and then power it up. Gently glide the stud finder in a slow, straight horizontal line until the device makes a noise or flashes. Then, slowly move the stud finder in the opposite direction to confirm the stud’s exact location.<ref>https://mrgrabbar.com/blogs/grab-bar-installation-tips-and-tricks/the-ultimate-guide-to-using-a-stud-finder-like-a-pro?srsltid=AfmBOor3PmDrx5_4EWzI9mRrWXfUqnM1h07QTO5InDEolZaSpVp4rw_V</ref> Sketch an “X” to mark the stud, and continue searching and marking until you’ve found all the wall studs in your AC’s proposed spot.[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 2 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* There’s about {{convert|16|in|cm|abbr=on}} of space between wall studs in most modern homes. If your house is on the older side, the wall studs may actually be {{convert|24|in|cm|abbr=on}} apart.<ref>https://mrgrabbar.com/blogs/grab-bar-installation-tips-and-tricks/the-ultimate-guide-to-using-a-stud-finder-like-a-pro?srsltid=AfmBOor3PmDrx5_4EWzI9mRrWXfUqnM1h07QTO5InDEolZaSpVp4rw_V</ref>",
                    "#Sketch the rectangular outline for your AC’s wall sleeve. Inspect the box or packaging that your wall AC and jot down the dimensions of the unit. Then, grab a ruler or tape measure and mark those exact dimensions onto the wall. Try to keep your lines as straight and even as possible.<ref>https://www.hvachowto.com/how-to-install-a-through-the-wall-air-conditioner-sleeve/</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 3 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Measure the top line of your biggest rectangular outline with a level. Hold the level flush against this upper line and see if it’s completely straight. If the line is slightly off-kilter, re-draw it on your wall, using the level as a reference.<ref>https://www.hvachowto.com/how-to-install-a-through-the-wall-air-conditioner-sleeve/</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 5 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Cut out the outlined section of the wall and any insulation with a utility knife. Carefully saw through the largest rectangular outline on your wall. Pull out the rectangle of drywall, and then cut out any visible insulation from the opening.<ref>https://www.hvachowto.com/how-to-install-a-through-the-wall-air-conditioner-sleeve/</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Remove any wall studs within the opening with a reciprocating handsaw. Slice along the top and bottom of the studs and remove the part that intersects with your opening.<ref>https://www.hvachowto.com/how-to-install-a-through-the-wall-air-conditioner-sleeve/</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 9 Version 3.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Mark the wall studs with a [[Find a Stud|stud finder]]. Arrange the device so it’s completely flush with the wall, and then power it up. Gently glide the stud finder in a slow, straight horizontal line until the device makes a noise or flashes. Then, slowly move the stud finder in the opposite direction to confirm the stud’s exact location.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/how-to-use-a-stud-finder/</ref> Sketch an “X” to mark the stud, and continue searching and marking until you’ve found all the wall studs in your AC’s proposed spot.[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 2 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* There’s about {{convert|16|in|cm|abbr=on}} of space between wall studs in most modern homes. If your house is on the older side, the wall studs may actually be {{convert|24|in|cm|abbr=on}} apart.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/how-to-use-a-stud-finder/</ref>",
                    "#Sketch the rectangular outline for your AC’s wall sleeve. Inspect the box or packaging that your wall AC and jot down the dimensions of the unit. Then, grab a ruler or tape measure and mark those exact dimensions onto the wall. Try to keep your lines as straight and even as possible.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=23s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 3 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Measure the top line of your biggest rectangular outline with a level. Hold the level flush against this upper line and see if it’s completely straight. If the line is slightly off-kilter, re-draw it on your wall, using the level as a reference.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=27s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 5 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Cut out the outlined section of the wall and any insulation with a utility knife. Carefully saw through the largest rectangular outline on your wall. Pull out the rectangle of drywall, and then cut out any visible insulation from the opening.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=32s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Remove any wall studs within the opening with a reciprocating handsaw. Slice along the top and bottom of the studs and remove the part that intersects with your opening.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=1m40s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 9 Version 3.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
