        {
            "id": "30246408",
            "user": "Wikivisual",
            "timestamp": "20:01, 27 January 2022",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(11,419 bytes)",
            "change": 1031,
            "summary": "(Saving new step-by-step photos)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Choose a wall that borders the outside of your home and has no pipes or wires. Wall sleeves are generally about {{convert|15|in|cm|abbr=on}} tall, {{convert|26|in|cm|abbr=on}} wide, and {{convert|16|in|cm|abbr=on}} deep. Select an exterior wall that doesn’t have any plumbing or electrical wiring, and choose a spot near an electrical outlet you can plug the unit into.<ref>https://todayshomeowner.com/conditioners-from-maintenance-buying/2/</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 1 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Mark the wall studs with a [[Find a Stud|stud finder]]. Arrange the device so it’s completely flush with the wall, and then power it up. Gently glide the stud finder in a slow, straight horizontal line until the device makes a noise or flashes. Then, slowly move the stud finder in the opposite direction to confirm the stud’s exact location.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/how-to-use-a-stud-finder/</ref> Sketch an “X” to mark the stud, and continue searching and marking until you’ve found all the wall studs in your AC’s proposed spot.[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 2 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Sketch the rectangular outline for your AC’s wall sleeve. Inspect the box or packaging that your wall AC and jot down the dimensions of the unit. Then, grab a ruler or tape measure and mark those exact dimensions onto the wall. Try to keep your lines as straight and even as possible.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=23s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 3 Version 4.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Draw a larger outline around your first rectangular sketch. Measure out and sketch this second rectangle so it’s about {{convert|2.5|in|cm|abbr=on}} bigger than your original outline.<ref>https://americanhomewater.com/through-the-wall-air-conditioner-installation/</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 4 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Measure the top line of your biggest rectangular outline with a level. Hold the level flush against this upper line and see if it’s completely straight. If the line is slightly off-kilter, re-draw it on your wall, using the level as a reference.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=27s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 5 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Shut off the power to the room you’ll be working in. While you shouldn’t be cutting through any wires or cables to install your AC, you still don’t want to risk getting a nasty shock during your renovations. To be safe, turn off the corresponding switches on your home circuit breaker before getting started.[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 6 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Slide on work gloves, a dust mask, and safety glasses before you get started. As you open up your wall, you’ll have to remove insulation from a select portion of the wall. Insulation is often made with fiberglass, which isn’t safe to handle with your bare hands. Make sure your eyes, nose, mouth, and skin are all protected before you get started.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/insulation/21019234/cutting-batts-of-fiberglass-insulation</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 7 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Cut out the outlined section of the wall and any insulation with a utility knife. Carefully saw through the largest rectangular outline on your wall. Pull out the rectangle of drywall, and then cut out any visible insulation from the opening.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=32s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 8 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Remove any wall studs within the opening with a reciprocating handsaw. Slice along the top and bottom of the studs and remove the part that intersects with your opening.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=1m40s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 9 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Drill a hole through each corner of the inner wall opening. Nestle your drill bit in each corner and drill all the way through the rest of the wall. Repeat this process with all 4 corners; when you’re finished, you should see 4 distinct dots on the outside of your home.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=1m15s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 10 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Cut out the exterior portion of the wall using a circular saw. Draw 4 lines around the drilled holes that exactly match the dimensions of your interior wall opening. Then, carefully slice through the outlined siding with a circular saw.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-WbVOjYuhuk&t=1m3s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 11.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Adjust the outer opening with an electric handsaw so it matches the inner opening. Even with careful measurements, your exterior opening might not be the exact same size as the interior opening. Make small adjustments with a handheld electric saw until both openings are totally even.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AJnHfe0tcnY&t=6m35s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 12 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Build a frame to hold your sleeve and AC in place. Measure both the length and height of your wall opening. Then, cut out two {{convert|2|by|4|in|cm|abbr=on}} planks that match this length measurement, and another 2 that match the height. Position these 4 planks inside of the wall opening and nail them into place.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-WbVOjYuhuk&t=1m56s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 13 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Insert the sleeve into your wall. If you have a disassembled sleeve, put it together by connecting all 4 sides and following the directions on the packaging. Slide the sleeve into the opening so the edges are flush against the inside of your wall.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AJnHfe0tcnY&t=7m53s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 14 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Slide the air conditioner into the sleeve. Lift the air conditioner and place the end into the wall sleeve. Push on the front of the unit to slide it into the sleeve. If it’s a tight fit, wiggle the unit as you push to help slide it into place.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JA8UgDNommI&t=6m20s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 15.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Stuff foam insulation strips into the gap around the unit. If there’s a gap between your air conditioning unit and the sleeve, it can affect how well your unit functions. Take strips of foam insulation and stuff them into the space to help seal it.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=3m14s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 16.jpg|center]]",
                    "#[[Caulk|Caulk]] around the outside portion of the sleeve to seal it in place. Squeeze a line of silicone-based caulk along the edges and in any holes or gaps and allow it to fully dry to create a tight seal.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=3m33s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 17.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Choose a wall that borders the outside of your home and has no pipes or wires. Wall sleeves are generally about {{convert|15|in|cm|abbr=on}} tall, {{convert|26|in|cm|abbr=on}} wide, and {{convert|16|in|cm|abbr=on}} deep. Select an exterior wall that doesn’t have any plumbing or electrical wiring, and choose a spot near an electrical outlet you can plug the unit into.<ref>https://todayshomeowner.com/conditioners-from-maintenance-buying/2/</ref>",
                    "#Mark the wall studs with a [[Find a Stud|stud finder]]. Arrange the device so it’s completely flush with the wall, and then power it up. Gently glide the stud finder in a slow, straight horizontal line until the device makes a noise or flashes. Then, slowly move the stud finder in the opposite direction to confirm the stud’s exact location.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/how-to-use-a-stud-finder/</ref> Sketch an “X” to mark the stud, and continue searching and marking until you’ve found all the wall studs in your AC’s proposed spot.",
                    "#Sketch the rectangular outline for your AC’s wall sleeve. Inspect the box or packaging that your wall AC and jot down the dimensions of the unit. Then, grab a ruler or tape measure and mark those exact dimensions onto the wall. Try to keep your lines as straight and even as possible.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=23s</ref>",
                    "#Draw a larger outline around your first rectangular sketch. Measure out and sketch this second rectangle so it’s about {{convert|2.5|in|cm|abbr=on}} bigger than your original outline.<ref>https://americanhomewater.com/through-the-wall-air-conditioner-installation/</ref>",
                    "#Measure the top line of your biggest rectangular outline with a level. Hold the level flush against this upper line and see if it’s completely straight. If the line is slightly off-kilter, re-draw it on your wall, using the level as a reference.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=27s</ref>",
                    "#Shut off the power to the room you’ll be working in. While you shouldn’t be cutting through any wires or cables to install your AC, you still don’t want to risk getting a nasty shock during your renovations. To be safe, turn off the corresponding switches on your home circuit breaker before getting started.",
                    "#Slide on work gloves, a dust mask, and safety glasses before you get started. As you open up your wall, you’ll have to remove insulation from a select portion of the wall. Insulation is often made with fiberglass, which isn’t safe to handle with your bare hands. Make sure your eyes, nose, mouth, and skin are all protected before you get started.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/insulation/21019234/cutting-batts-of-fiberglass-insulation</ref>",
                    "#Cut out the outlined section of the wall and any insulation with a utility knife. Carefully saw through the largest rectangular outline on your wall. Pull out the rectangle of drywall, and then cut out any visible insulation from the opening.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=32s</ref>",
                    "#Remove any wall studs within the opening with a reciprocating handsaw. Slice along the top and bottom of the studs and remove the part that intersects with your opening.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=1m40s</ref>",
                    "#Drill a hole through each corner of the inner wall opening. Nestle your drill bit in each corner and drill all the way through the rest of the wall. Repeat this process with all 4 corners; when you’re finished, you should see 4 distinct dots on the outside of your home.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=1m15s</ref>",
                    "#Cut out the exterior portion of the wall using a circular saw. Draw 4 lines around the drilled holes that exactly match the dimensions of your interior wall opening. Then, carefully slice through the outlined siding with a circular saw.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-WbVOjYuhuk&t=1m3s</ref>",
                    "#Adjust the outer opening with an electric handsaw so it matches the inner opening. Even with careful measurements, your exterior opening might not be the exact same size as the interior opening. Make small adjustments with a handheld electric saw until both openings are totally even.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AJnHfe0tcnY&t=6m35s</ref>",
                    "#Build a frame to hold your sleeve and AC in place. Measure both the length and height of your wall opening. Then, cut out two {{convert|2|by|4|in|cm|abbr=on}} planks that match this length measurement, and another 2 that match the height. Position these 4 planks inside of the wall opening and nail them into place.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-WbVOjYuhuk&t=1m56s</ref>",
                    "#Insert the sleeve into your wall. If you have a disassembled sleeve, put it together by connecting all 4 sides and following the directions on the packaging. Slide the sleeve into the opening so the edges are flush against the inside of your wall.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AJnHfe0tcnY&t=7m53s</ref>",
                    "#Slide the air conditioner into the sleeve. Lift the air conditioner and place the end into the wall sleeve. Push on the front of the unit to slide it into the sleeve. If it’s a tight fit, wiggle the unit as you push to help slide it into place.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JA8UgDNommI&t=6m20s</ref>",
                    "#Stuff foam insulation strips into the gap around the unit. If there’s a gap between your air conditioning unit and the sleeve, it can affect how well your unit functions. Take strips of foam insulation and stuff them into the space to help seal it.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=3m14s</ref> [[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#[[Caulk|Caulk]] around the outside portion of the sleeve to seal it in place. Squeeze a line of silicone-based caulk along the edges and in any holes or gaps and allow it to fully dry to create a tight seal.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=3m33s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 3 Version 3.jpg|center]]"
                ]
            }
        },
