            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "#Choose a wall that borders the outside of your home and has no pipes or wires. Wall sleeves are generally about {{convert|15|in|cm|abbr=on}} tall, {{convert|26|in|cm|abbr=on}} wide, and {{convert|16|in|cm|abbr=on}} deep. Select an exterior wall that doesn’t have any plumbing or electrical wiring, and choose a spot near an electrical outlet you can plug the unit into.<ref>https://todayshomeowner.com/conditioners-from-maintenance-buying/2/</ref>",
                    "#Measure the top line of your biggest rectangular outline with a level. Hold the level flush against this upper line and see if it’s completely straight. If the line is slightly off-kilter, re-draw it on your wall, using the level as a reference.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=27s</ref>",
                    "#Remove any wall studs within the opening with a reciprocating handsaw. Slice along the top and bottom of the studs and remove the part that intersects with your opening.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=1m40s</ref>",
                    "#* Wall studs resemble vertical planks running through your walls.",
                    "#Drill a hole through each corner of the inner wall opening. Nestle your drill bit in each corner and drill all the way through the rest of the wall. Repeat this process with all 4 corners; when you’re finished, you should see 4 distinct dots on the outside of your home.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=1m15s</ref>",
                    "#* When put together, these 4 dots create the rectangular outline of your wall opening.",
                    "#* Home improvement experts don’t recommend a specific drill bit size for this project–just make sure that your bit is large enough to create noticeable holes through your exterior wall.",
                    "#* The wall sleeve comes with your AC unit.",
                    "#Stuff foam insulation strips into the gap around the unit. If there’s a gap between your air conditioning unit and the sleeve, it can affect how well your unit functions. Take strips of foam insulation and stuff them into the space to help seal it.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=3m14s</ref> [[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* At this point, you can turn the power back on."
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Choose an interior and exterior wall to install your air conditioner sleeve. Wall sleeves are generally about {{convert|15|in|cm|abbr=on}} tall, {{convert|26|in|cm|abbr=on}} wide, and {{convert|16|in|cm|abbr=on}} deep. Select an exterior wall that doesn’t have any plumbing or electrical wiring, and choose a spot near an electrical outlet you can plug the unit into.<ref>https://todayshomeowner.com/conditioners-from-maintenance-buying/2/</ref>",
                    "#Inspect the top line of your rectangular outline with a level. Hold the level flush against this upper line and see if it’s completely straight. If the line is slightly off-kilter, re-draw it on your wall, using the level as a reference.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=27s</ref>",
                    "#Remove any wall studs within the opening with a reciprocating handsaw. Slice along the top and bottom of the studs and remove the {{convert|2|by|4|in|cm|abbr=on}} stud plank from the wall.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=1m40s</ref>",
                    "#Drill a hole through each corner of the inner wall opening. Nestle your drill bit in each corner and drill all the way through the rest of the wall. Repeat this process with all 4 corners; when you’re finished, you should see a drilled rectangular outline along the outside of your home.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=1m15s</ref>",
                    "#* This way, you’ll have an easier time marking and removing the outer section of your wall.",
                    "#Put foam into the gap around the unit. If there’s a gap between your air conditioning unit and the sleeve, it can affect how well your unit functions. Take strips of foam insulation and stuff them into the space to help seal it.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=3m14s</ref> [[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Turn your power back on. Go back to the circuit breaker and toggle the corresponding power switches to the room your AC is in."
                ]
            }
        },
