            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Looking to cool a room without a clunky A/C unit sticking out of a window? Well, look no further than an inwall air conditioner! Also known as through-the-wall or wall units, inwall air conditioners are installed directly into your wall to provide cool air without being as noticeable as a window unit. They’re also relatively easy to install on your own if you have the right tools and some experience with DIY home improvement projects. To make your job easier, we’ve covered the entire installation process from start to finish, so you can cool off as soon as possible.",
                    "===Measuring and Marking the Location===",
                    "#Choose an interior and exterior wall to install your air conditioner sleeve. Wall sleeves are generally about {{convert|15|in|cm|abbr=on}} tall, {{convert|26|in|cm|abbr=on}} wide, and {{convert|16|in|cm|abbr=on}} deep. Select an exterior wall that doesn’t have any plumbing or electrical wiring, and choose a spot near an electrical outlet you can plug the unit into.<ref>https://todayshomeowner.com/conditioners-from-maintenance-buying/2/</ref>",
                    "#* A wall sleeve is the casing that supports your air conditioner as it slides into the wall.",
                    "#* The wall you choose should border the outside of your home–it shouldn’t be an interior wall between 2 indoor rooms.",
                    "#* Many experts suggest mounting your AC around {{convert|1|to|5|ft|m|abbr=on}} off the floor. If you aren’t sure, consider speaking with an installation professional about placement.",
                    "#Mark the wall studs with a [[Find a Stud|stud finder]]. Arrange the device so it’s completely flush with the wall, and then power it up. Gently glide the stud finder in a slow, straight horizontal line until the device makes a noise or flashes. Then, slowly move the stud finder in the opposite direction to confirm the stud’s exact location.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/how-to-use-a-stud-finder/</ref> Sketch an “X” to mark the stud, and continue searching and marking until you’ve found all the wall studs in your AC’s proposed spot.",
                    "#* There’s about {{convert|16|in|cm|abbr=on}} of space between wall studs in most modern homes. If your house is on the older side, the wall studs may actually be {{convert|24|in|cm|abbr=on}} apart.<ref>https://www.bobvila.com/articles/how-to-use-a-stud-finder/</ref>",
                    "#Sketch the rectangular outline for your AC’s wall sleeve. Inspect the box or packaging that your wall AC and jot down the dimensions of the unit. Then, grab a ruler or tape measure and mark those exact dimensions onto the wall. Try to keep your lines as straight and even as possible.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=23s</ref>",
                    "#*If you can’t find any dimensions, measure the sleeve itself with a ruler or tape measure.",
                    "#Draw a larger outline around your first rectangular sketch. Measure out and sketch this second rectangle so it’s about {{convert|2.5|in|cm|abbr=on}} bigger than your original outline.<ref>https://americanhomewater.com/through-the-wall-air-conditioner-installation/</ref>",
                    "#* This second, larger outline gives you enough clearance to install a supportive frame around your AC sleeve.",
                    "#Inspect the top line of your rectangular outline with a level. Hold the level flush against this upper line and see if it’s completely straight. If the line is slightly off-kilter, re-draw it on your wall, using the level as a reference.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=27s</ref>",
                    "",
                    "#Slide on work gloves, a dust mask, and safety glasses before you get started. As you open up your wall, you’ll have to remove insulation from a select portion of the wall. Insulation is often made with fiberglass, which isn’t safe to handle with your bare hands. Make sure your eyes, nose, mouth, and skin are all protected before you get started.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/insulation/21019234/cutting-batts-of-fiberglass-insulation</ref>",
                    "#Cut out the outlined section of the wall and any insulation with a utility knife. Carefully saw through the largest rectangular outline on your wall. Pull out the rectangle of drywall, and then cut out any visible insulation from the opening.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=32s</ref>",
                    "#*  You can also lightly slice around the edge of the outline with a drywall knife and knock out the bulk of the rectangle with a hammer.<ref>https://americanhomewater.com/through-the-wall-air-conditioner-installation/</ref>",
                    "#* Feel free to sand the edges of the wall if you want to smooth them out.",
                    "#Drill a hole through each corner of the inner wall opening. Nestle your drill bit in each corner and drill all the way through the rest of the wall. Repeat this process with all 4 corners; when you’re finished, you should see a drilled rectangular outline along the outside of your home.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=1m15s</ref>",
                    "#* This way, you’ll have an easier time marking and removing the outer section of your wall.",
                    "#* If your home’s exterior is made of stucco, it may help to carve a {{convert|1/4|in|cm|abbr=on}} thick outline around your sketch first (using a circular saw). Then, you may have to manually chisel the stucco so you can access and cut away the chicken wire underneath.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-WbVOjYuhuk&t=1m12s</ref>",
                    "#Adjust the outer opening with an electric handsaw so it matches the inner opening. Even with careful measurements, your exterior opening might not be the exact same size as the interior opening. Make small adjustments with a handheld electric saw until both openings are totally even.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AJnHfe0tcnY&t=6m35s</ref>",
                    "#Slide the air conditioner into the sleeve. Lift the air conditioner and place the end into the wall sleeve. Push on the front of the unit to slide it into the sleeve. If it’s a tight fit, wiggle the unit as you push to help slide it into place.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=JA8UgDNommI&t=6m20s</ref>",
                    "#* Some air conditioning units may come with a frame-like border that goes around the front of the device.",
                    "#Put foam into the gap around the unit. If there’s a gap between your air conditioning unit and the sleeve, it can affect how well your unit functions. Take strips of foam insulation and stuff them into the space to help seal it.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=3m14s</ref> [[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#* This is a good time to [[Do Drywall Repair|make any repairs and fixes to your interior drywall, too]].",
                    "#Turn your power back on. Go back to the circuit breaker and toggle the corresponding power switches to the room your AC is in.",
                    "*Drywall knife (optional)"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Looking to cool a room without a clunky A/C unit sticking out of a window? Well, look no further than an inwall air conditioner! Also known as through-the-wall or wall units, inwall air conditioners are installed directly into your wall to provide cool air without being as noticeable as a window unit. They’re also relatively easy to install on your own if you have the right tools and some experience with home renovations. To make your job easier, we’ve covered the entire installation process from start to finish, so you can cool off as soon as possible.",
                    "#Choose an exterior wall to install your air conditioner sleeve. Wall sleeves are generally about {{convert|15|in|cm|abbr=on}} tall, {{convert|26|in|cm|abbr=on}} wide, and {{convert|16|in|cm|abbr=on}} deep. Select an exterior wall that doesn’t have any plumbing or electrical wiring, and choose a spot near an electrical outlet you can plug the unit into.<ref>https://todayshomeowner.com/conditioners-from-maintenance-buying/2/</ref>",
                    "#* A wall sleeve is the casing that supports your air conditioner as it slides into the wall.",
                    "#Mark the wall studs and opening size for the wall sleeve. [[Find a Stud|Use a stud finder]] to find your wall studs and mark them with a pencil. Then, use a ruler or tape measure to measure and mark a rectangle on your wall that matches the size of your sleeve.<ref>https://youtu.be/A_R71xYPyks?t=11</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Your sleeve should have size measurements on the packaging. If it doesn’t, measure the sleeve itself with a ruler or tape measure.",
                    "#Check the top line of your sleeve outline to ensure that it’s level. Hold the level so it’s completely flush with the top line of your rectangular sleeve sketch. If the line isn’t quite straight, re-draw it using the level as your guide.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=27s</ref>",
                    "#Slide on work gloves, a dust mask, and safety glasses before you get started. To install an air conditioner sleeve, you’ll have to remove insulation from a select portion of the wall. Insulation is often made with fiberglass, which isn’t safe to handle with your bare hands. Make sure your eyes, nose, mouth, and skin are all protected before you get started.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/insulation/21019234/cutting-batts-of-fiberglass-insulation</ref>",
                    "#Cut out the drywall and remove the insulation. Grab a utility knife and carefully saw through the rectangular outline on your wall. Pull out the rectangle of drywall, and cut out any visible insulation from the opening.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=32s</ref>",
                    "#*You can also sand the edges of the wall if you want to smooth them out.",
                    "#Drill a hole through 4 corners of the wall opening to create an exterior reference point. Nestle your drill bit in each corner and drill all the way through the rest of the wall. Repeat this process with all 4 corners; when you’re finished, you should see a drilled rectangular outline along the outside of your home.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=1m15s</ref>",
                    "#* If the your home’s exterior is made of stucco, it may help to carve a {{convert|1/4|in|cm|abbr=on}} thick outline around your sketch first (using a circular saw). Then, you may have to manually chisel the stucco so you can access and cut away the chicken wire underneath.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-WbVOjYuhuk&t=1m12s</ref>",
                    "#Fine-tune the outer opening with an electric handsaw. This way, it matches up with the interior opening. Even with careful measurements, your exterior opening might not be the exact same size as the interior opening. Make small adjustments with a handheld electric saw until both openings are totally even.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AJnHfe0tcnY&t=6m35s</ref>",
                    "#* It may take a little bit of elbow grease to finesse the sleeve into the opening at first.",
                    "#Slide the air conditioner into the sleeve. Lift the air conditioner and place the end into the wall sleeve. Push on the front of the unit to slide it into the sleeve. If it’s a tight fit, wiggle the unit as you push to help slide it into place.<ref>https://youtu.be/w2_Nzl0eZjc?t=140</ref>",
                    "#Turn your power back on. Go back to circuit breaker and toggle the corresponding power switches to the room your AC is in.",
                    "#Put foam into the gap around the unit and install your frame. If there’s a gap between your air conditioning unit and the sleeve, it can affect how well your unit functions. Take strips of foam insulation and stuff them into the space to help seal it. Your inwall air conditioner unit also includes a frame that covers the front. Put together the frame of your air conditioner by connecting both sides together and then attach it to the front of the unit.<ref>https://youtu.be/OhoxHHoT57w?t=68</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Once your unit is installed, you can plug it in and turn it on to test it out!",
                    "#* This a good time to [[Do Drywall Repair|make any repairs and fixes to your interior drywall, too]]."
                ]
            }
        },
