        {
            "id": "30214904",
            "user": "Seymour Edits",
            "timestamp": "20:29, 18 January 2022",
            "anon": false,
            "size": "(8,673 bytes)",
            "change": 1123,
            "summary": "(Converted to Parts; added clarifying steps to flesh out article)",
            "is_minor": false,
            "is_expert": false,
            "status": "active",
            "contribution_type": "major_addition",
            "exact_contribution": {
                "added": [
                    "Looking to cool a room without a clunky A/C unit sticking out of a window? Well, look no further than an inwall air conditioner! Also known as through-the-wall or wall units, inwall air conditioners are installed directly into your wall to provide cool air without being as noticeable as a window unit. They’re also relatively easy to install on your own if you have the right tools and some experience with home renovations. To make your job easier, we’ve covered the entire installation process from start to finish, so you can cool off as soon as possible.",
                    "",
                    "===Creating the Wall Opening===",
                    "#Choose an exterior wall to install your air conditioner sleeve. Wall sleeves are generally about {{convert|15|in|cm|abbr=on}} tall, {{convert|26|in|cm|abbr=on}} wide, and {{convert|16|in|cm|abbr=on}} deep. Select an exterior wall that doesn’t have any plumbing or electrical wiring, and choose a spot near an electrical outlet you can plug the unit into.<ref>https://todayshomeowner.com/conditioners-from-maintenance-buying/2/</ref>",
                    "#* A wall sleeve is the casing that supports your air conditioner as it slides into the wall.",
                    "#Mark the wall studs and opening size for the wall sleeve. [[Find a Stud|Use a stud finder]] to find your wall studs and mark them with a pencil. Then, use a ruler or tape measure to measure and mark a rectangle on your wall that matches the size of your sleeve.<ref>https://youtu.be/A_R71xYPyks?t=11</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Shut off the power to the room you’ll be working in. While you shouldn’t be cutting through any wires or cables to install your AC, you still don’t want to risk getting a nasty shock during your renovations. To be safe, turn off the corresponding switches on your home circuit breaker before getting started.",
                    "#Check the top line of your sleeve outline to ensure that it’s level. Hold the level so it’s completely flush with the top line of your rectangular sleeve sketch. If the line isn’t quite straight, re-draw it using the level as your guide.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=27s</ref>",
                    "#Slide on work gloves, a dust mask, and safety glasses before you get started. To install an air conditioner sleeve, you’ll have to remove insulation from a select portion of the wall. Insulation is often made with fiberglass, which isn’t safe to handle with your bare hands. Make sure your eyes, nose, mouth, and skin are all protected before you get started.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/insulation/21019234/cutting-batts-of-fiberglass-insulation</ref>",
                    "#* To be extra safe, slip on a pair of long pants and a long-sleeved shirt so you don’t get any fiberglass on your arms or legs.",
                    "#Cut out the drywall and remove the insulation. Grab a utility knife and carefully saw through the rectangular outline on your wall. Pull out the rectangle of drywall, and cut out any visible insulation from the opening.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=32s</ref>",
                    "#Remove any wall studs within the opening with a reciprocating handsaw. Slice along the top and bottom of the studs and remove the {{convert|2|by|4|in|cm|abbr=on}} stud plank from the wall.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=1m40s</ref>",
                    "#Drill a hole through 4 corners of the wall opening to create an exterior reference point. Nestle your drill bit in each corner and drill all the way through the rest of the wall. Repeat this process with all 4 corners; when you’re finished, you should see a drilled rectangular outline along the outside of your home.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=1m15s</ref>",
                    "#Cut out the exterior portion of the wall using a circular saw. Draw 4 lines around the drilled holes that exactly match the dimensions of your interior wall opening. Then, carefully slice through the outlined siding with a circular saw.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-WbVOjYuhuk&t=1m3s</ref>",
                    "#* If the your home’s exterior is made of stucco, it may help to carve a {{convert|1/4|in|cm|abbr=on}} thick outline around your sketch first (using a circular saw). Then, you may have to manually chisel the stucco so you can access and cut away the chicken wire underneath.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-WbVOjYuhuk&t=1m12s</ref>",
                    "#Fine-tune the outer opening with an electric handsaw. This way, it matches up with the interior opening. Even with careful measurements, your exterior opening might not be the exact same size as the interior opening. Make small adjustments with a handheld electric saw until both openings are totally even.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AJnHfe0tcnY&t=6m35s</ref>",
                    "",
                    "===Installing the Wall Sleeve and AC===",
                    "#Build a frame to hold your sleeve and AC in place. Measure both the length and height of your wall opening. Then, cut out two {{convert|2|by|4|in|cm|abbr=on}} planks that match this length measurement, and another 2 that match the height. Position these 4 planks inside of the wall opening and nail them into place.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=-WbVOjYuhuk&t=1m56s</ref>",
                    "#* If you’re having trouble nailing the frame together, use corner brackets or wood glue instead.",
                    "#Insert the sleeve into your wall. If you have a disassembled sleeve, put it together by connecting all 4 sides and following the directions on the packaging. Slide the sleeve into the opening so the edges are flush against the inside of your wall.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=AJnHfe0tcnY&t=7m53s</ref>",
                    "#* It may take a little bit of elbow grease to finesse the sleeve into the opening at first.",
                    "#Turn your power back on. Go back to circuit breaker and toggle the corresponding power switches to the room your AC is in.",
                    "#* This a good time to [[Do Drywall Repair|make any repairs and fixes to your interior drywall, too]].",
                    "#[[Caulk|Caulk]] around the outside portion of the sleeve to seal it in place. Squeeze a line of silicone-based caulk along the edges and in any holes or gaps and allow it to fully dry to create a tight seal.<ref>https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=A_R71xYPyks&t=3m33s</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 3 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Check the package of the caulk for specific drying times and wait until the caulk has fully dried and hardened before you install your air conditioning unit.",
                    "",
                    "==Things You’ll Need==",
                    "*Measuring tape",
                    "*Stud finder",
                    "*Pencil",
                    "*Level",
                    "*Work gloves",
                    "*Dust mask",
                    "*Safety glasses",
                    "*Utility knife",
                    "*Reciprocating handsaw",
                    "*Drill",
                    "*Circular saw",
                    "*Four {{convert|2|by|4|in|cm|abbr=on}} planks",
                    "*Nails",
                    "*Hammer",
                    "*Air conditioner sleeve",
                    "*Foam insulation",
                    "*Silicone-based caulk",
                    "*Chisel (optional)",
                    "",
                    "*For the highest quality installation, hire a professional to do the job. They’ll be able to cut out your wall and seal any gaps so your unit is running as smoothly and efficiently as possible. Costs can vary depending on how long it takes to get the job done, but generally, costs are below $300.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/heating-cooling/21015186/how-to-choose-an-air-conditioner</ref>",
                    "__PARTS__"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "Looking to cool a room without a clunky A/C unit sticking out of a window? Well, look no further than an inwall air conditioner! Also known as through-the-wall or wall units, inwall air conditioners are installed directly into your wall to provide cool air without being as noticeable as a window unit. They’re also relatively easy to install on your own if you have the right tools and some experience with home renovations. To make your job easier, we’ve answered some of the most common questions that folks have about what it takes to install an inwall air conditioner.",
                    "===How do you install an inwall air conditioner sleeve?===",
                    "#Choose a wall and mark the studs and opening size for the sleeve. Wall sleeves are generally about {{convert|15|in|cm}} tall, {{convert|26|in|cm}} wide, and {{convert|16|in|cm}} deep. Select an exterior wall that doesn’t have any plumbing or electrical wiring, and choose a spot near an electrical outlet you can plug the unit into.<ref>https://todayshomeowner.com/conditioners-from-maintenance-buying/2/</ref> Use a stud finder to find your wall studs and mark them with a pencil. Then, use a ruler or tape measure to measure and mark a rectangle on your wall that matches the size of your sleeve about {{convert|1-5|ft|m}} above the floor.<ref>https://youtu.be/A_R71xYPyks?t=11</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Installing the unit about {{convert|1-5|ft|m}} above the floor will help minimize the dust that the air filter collects and prevent condensation.<ref>https://homeairguides.com/how-to-install-a-through-the-wall-air-conditioner/</ref>",
                    "#Cut a space through the wall with a reciprocating saw. Cut along the lines you marked as cleanly and evenly as possible. Cut all the way through the inside and outside of the wall to create an opening that will hold your sleeve. If there are any wall studs in the space, you’ll need to cut through them as well.<ref>https://youtu.be/A_R71xYPyks?t=152</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Slide the sleeve into your wall and caulk the sides to seal it. If you have a disassembled sleeve, put it together by connecting all 4 sides and following the directions on the packaging. Slide the sleeve into the opening so the edges are flush against the inside of your wall. Then, apply a silicone-based caulk along the edges and in any holes or gaps and allow it to fully dry to create a tight seal.<ref>https://www.hvachowto.com/how-to-install-a-through-the-wall-air-conditioner-sleeve/</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 3 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Check the package of the caulk for specific drying times and wait until the caulk has fully dried and hardened before you install your air conditioning unit.",
                    "===How do you fit an inwall air conditioner into the sleeve?===",
                    "#*Inwall air conditioning units can be heavy. If you’re having trouble, try asking a friend to help you lift it.",
                    "===Are window and inwall air conditioners the same?===",
                    "#No, they are slightly different. The main difference is their location: window units are designed to be placed on a windowsill and inwall units are meant to be installed in an exterior wall. They’re both designed to provide cool air to a room, but window air conditioners can be less efficient and they take up window space. Inwall air conditioners can take more time to install, but they’ll also last longer and look less conspicuous than a window unit.<ref>https://homeairguides.com/how-to-install-a-through-the-wall-air-conditioner/</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 1 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*Additionally, both types cost around $250 USD but an inwall may cost more because of the installation process.<ref>https://www.homeadvisor.com/cost/heating-and-cooling/install-a-window-air-conditioner/</ref>",
                    "===Can you install a window air conditioner through a wall?===",
                    "#Yes, some units are designed to be installed in both. Some air conditioning units are designed specifically for window or wall use, and they’ll be labeled in their product description. Inwall units fit inside of a pre-installed sleeve, and if you have an air conditioning unit that’s designed for both inwall and window use, you can install it in your wall.<ref>https://youtu.be/OqwSORYS3Pk?t=27</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "===Do inwall air conditioners need a sleeve?===",
                    "#Yes, the sleeve is an essential part of the installation process. The sleeve, also known as a bracket, is a metal shell that fits into your wall to hold the unit and support its weight. Without a sleeve, there’s nothing to stabilize the air conditioner. The wall sleeve is also what allows you to install your unit into any type of wall. Additionally, the sleeve has vents that allow your unit to breathe and prevent overheating.<ref>https://todayshomeowner.com/conditioners-from-maintenance-buying/2/</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#*It’s really important that the vents of the air conditioner aren’t blocked so it functions properly.",
                    "===How much does it cost to install an air conditioner in the wall?===",
                    "#It costs between $175-$250 USD for the installation. For the highest quality installation, hire a professional to do the job. They’ll be able to cut out your wall and seal any gaps so your unit is running as smoothly and efficiently as possible. Costs can vary depending on how long it takes to get the job done, but generally, costs are below $300.<ref>https://www.thisoldhouse.com/heating-cooling/21015186/how-to-choose-an-air-conditioner</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 9 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "*A professionally installed inwall air conditioning unit is still much cheaper than a central air system. It may be worth the extra bucks to have it done right.",
                    "__QAM__"
                ]
            }
        },
