                "added": [
                    "#Choose a wall and mark the studs and opening size for the sleeve. Wall sleeves are generally about {{convert|15|in|cm}} tall, {{convert|26|in|cm}} wide, and {{convert|16|in|cm}} deep. Select an exterior wall that doesn’t have any plumbing or electrical wiring, and choose a spot near an electrical outlet you can plug the unit into.<ref>https://todayshomeowner.com/conditioners-from-maintenance-buying/2/</ref> Use a stud finder to find your wall studs and mark them with a pencil. Then, use a ruler or tape measure to measure and mark a rectangle on your wall that matches the size of your sleeve about {{convert|1-5|ft|m}} above the floor.<ref>https://youtu.be/A_R71xYPyks?t=11</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 1 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Cut a space through the wall with a reciprocating saw. Cut along the lines you marked as cleanly and evenly as possible. Cut all the way through the inside and outside of the wall to create an opening that will hold your sleeve. If there are any wall studs in the space, you’ll need to cut through them as well.<ref>https://youtu.be/A_R71xYPyks?t=152</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 2 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Slide the sleeve into your wall and caulk the sides to seal it. If you have a disassembled sleeve, put it together by connecting all 4 sides and following the directions on the packaging. Slide the sleeve into the opening so the edges are flush against the inside of your wall. Then, apply a silicone-based caulk along the edges and in any holes or gaps and allow it to fully dry to create a tight seal.<ref>https://www.hvachowto.com/how-to-install-a-through-the-wall-air-conditioner-sleeve/</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 3 Version 3.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Put foam into the gap around the unit and install your frame. If there’s a gap between your air conditioning unit and the sleeve, it can affect how well your unit functions. Take strips of foam insulation and stuff them into the space to help seal it. Your inwall air conditioner unit also includes a frame that covers the front. Put together the frame of your air conditioner by connecting both sides together and then attach it to the front of the unit.<ref>https://youtu.be/OhoxHHoT57w?t=68</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 5 Version 2.jpg|center]]"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#Choose a wall and mark the studs and opening size for the sleeve. Wall sleeves are generally about {{convert|15|in|cm}} tall, {{convert|26|in|cm}} wide, and {{convert|16|in|cm}} deep. Select an exterior wall that doesn’t have any plumbing or electrical wiring, and choose a spot near an electrical outlet you can plug the unit into.<ref>https://todayshomeowner.com/conditioners-from-maintenance-buying/2/</ref> Use a stud finder to find your wall studs and mark them with a pencil. Then, use a ruler or tape measure to measure and mark a rectangle on your wall that matches the size of your sleeve about {{convert|1-5|ft|m}} above the floor.<ref>https://youtu.be/A_R71xYPyks?t=11</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Cut a space through the wall with a reciprocating saw. Cut along the lines you marked as cleanly and evenly as possible. Cut all the way through the inside and outside of the wall to create an opening that will hold your sleeve. If there are any wall studs in the space, you’ll need to cut through them as well.<ref>https://youtu.be/A_R71xYPyks?t=152</ref>",
                    "#Slide the sleeve into your wall and caulk the sides to seal it. If you have a disassembled sleeve, put it together by connecting all 4 sides and following the directions on the packaging. Slide the sleeve into the opening so the edges are flush against the inside of your wall. Then, apply a silicone-based caulk along the edges and in any holes or gaps and allow it to fully dry to create a tight seal.<ref>https://www.hvachowto.com/how-to-install-a-through-the-wall-air-conditioner-sleeve/</ref>",
                    "#Put foam into the gap around the unit and install your frame. If there’s a gap between your air conditioning unit and the sleeve, it can affect how well your unit functions. Take strips of foam insulation and stuff them into the space to help seal it. Your inwall air conditioner unit also includes a frame that covers the front. Put together the frame of your air conditioner by connecting both sides together and then attach it to the front of the unit.<ref>https://youtu.be/OhoxHHoT57w?t=68</ref>"
                ]
            }
        },
