                    "#Yes, some units are designed to be installed in both. Some air conditioning units are designed specifically for window or wall use, and they’ll be labeled in their product description. Inwall units fit inside of a pre-installed sleeve, and if you have an air conditioning unit that’s designed for both inwall and window use, you can install it in your wall.<ref>https://youtu.be/OqwSORYS3Pk?t=27</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 2 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Yes, the sleeve is an essential part of the installation process. The sleeve, also known as a bracket, is a metal shell that fits into your wall to hold the unit and support its weight. Without a sleeve, there’s nothing to stabilize the air conditioner. The wall sleeve is also what allows you to install your unit into any type of wall. Additionally, the sleeve has vents that allow your unit to breathe and prevent overheating.<ref>https://todayshomeowner.com/conditioners-from-maintenance-buying/2/</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 3 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Choose a wall and mark the studs and opening size for the sleeve. Wall sleeves are generally about {{convert|15|in|cm}} tall, {{convert|26|in|cm}} wide, and {{convert|16|in|cm}} deep. Select an exterior wall that doesn’t have any plumbing or electrical wiring, and choose a spot near an electrical outlet you can plug the unit into.<ref>https://todayshomeowner.com/conditioners-from-maintenance-buying/2/</ref> Use a stud finder to find your wall studs and mark them with a pencil. Then, use a ruler or tape measure to measure and mark a rectangle on your wall that matches the size of your sleeve about {{convert|1-5|ft|m}} above the floor.<ref>https://youtu.be/A_R71xYPyks?t=11</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 4 Version 2.jpg|center]]",
                    "#Slide the air conditioner into the sleeve. Lift the air conditioner and place the end into the wall sleeve. Push on the front of the unit to slide it into the sleeve. If it’s a tight fit, wiggle the unit as you push to help slide it into place.<ref>https://youtu.be/w2_Nzl0eZjc?t=140</ref>[[Image:Install an Inwall Air Conditioner Step 7.jpg|center]]",
