                "added": [
                    "#No, they are slightly different. The main difference is their location: window units are designed to be placed on a windowsill and inwall units are meant to be installed in an exterior wall. They’re both designed to provide cool air to a room, but window air conditioners can be less efficient and they take up window space. Inwall air conditioners can take more time to install, but they’ll also last longer and look less conspicuous than a window unit.<ref>https://homeairguides.com/how-to-install-a-through-the-wall-air-conditioner/</ref>",
                    "#*Additionally, both types cost around $250 USD but an inwall may cost more because of the installation process.<ref>https://www.homeadvisor.com/cost/heating-and-cooling/install-a-window-air-conditioner/</ref>",
                    "#Yes, the sleeve is an essential part of the installation process. The sleeve, also known as a bracket, is a metal shell that fits into your wall to hold the unit and support its weight. Without a sleeve, there’s nothing to stabilize the air conditioner. The wall sleeve is also what allows you to install your unit into any type of wall. Additionally, the sleeve has vents that allow your unit to breathe and prevent overheating.<ref>https://todayshomeowner.com/conditioners-from-maintenance-buying/2/</ref>",
                    "#Choose a wall and mark the studs and opening size for the sleeve. Wall sleeves are generally about {{convert|15|in|cm}} tall, {{convert|26|in|cm}} wide, and {{convert|16|in|cm}} deep. Select an exterior wall that doesn’t have any plumbing or electrical wiring, and choose a spot near an electrical outlet you can plug the unit into.<ref>https://todayshomeowner.com/conditioners-from-maintenance-buying/2/</ref> Use a stud finder to find your wall studs and mark them with a pencil. Then, use a ruler or tape measure to measure and mark a rectangle on your wall that matches the size of your sleeve about {{convert|1-5|ft|m}} above the floor.<ref>https://youtu.be/A_R71xYPyks?t=11</ref>",
                    "#*Installing the unit about {{convert|1-5|ft|m}} above the floor will help minimize the dust that the air filter collects and prevent condensation.<ref>https://homeairguides.com/how-to-install-a-through-the-wall-air-conditioner/</ref>",
                    "#Slide the sleeve into your wall and caulk the sides to seal it. If you have a disassembled sleeve, put it together by connecting all 4 sides and following the directions on the packaging. Slide the sleeve into the opening so the edges are flush against the inside of your wall. Then, apply a silicone-based caulk along the edges and in any holes or gaps and allow it to fully dry to create a tight seal.<ref>https://www.hvachowto.com/how-to-install-a-through-the-wall-air-conditioner-sleeve/</ref>",
                    "===How do you fit an inwall air conditioner into the sleeve?===",
                    "#Put foam into the gap around the unit and install your frame. If there’s a gap between your air conditioning unit and the sleeve, it can affect how well your unit functions. Take strips of foam insulation and stuff them into the space to help seal it. Your inwall air conditioner unit also includes a frame that covers the front. Put together the frame of your air conditioner by connecting both sides together and then attach it to the front of the unit.<ref>https://youtu.be/OhoxHHoT57w?t=68</ref>"
                ],
                "removed": [
                    "#No, they are slightly different. The main difference is their location: window units are designed to be placed on a windowsill and inwall units are meant to be installed in an exterior wall. They’re both designed to provide cool air to a room, but window air conditioners can be less efficient and they take up window space. Inwall air conditioners can take more time to install, but they’ll also last longer and look less conspicuous than a window unit.<ref>https://www.pickhvac.com/window-air-conditioner/through-the-wall/</ref>",
                    "#*Additionally, both types cost about the same, but an inwall may cost more because of the installation process.",
                    "#Yes, the sleeve is an essential part of the installation process. The sleeve helps hold the unit and support its weight. Without a sleeve, there’s nothing to stabilize the air conditioner. Additionally, the sleeve has vents on the backside and the sides of the air conditioner, which allow it to breathe and prevent overheating.<ref>https://todayshomeowner.com/conditioners-from-maintenance-buying/2/</ref>",
                    "#Choose a wall and mark the studs and opening size for the sleeve. Select an exterior wall that doesn’t have any plumbing or electrical wiring.<ref>https://todayshomeowner.com/conditioners-from-maintenance-buying/2/</ref> Use a stud finder to find your wall studs and mark them with a pencil. Then, use a ruler or tape measure to measure and mark a rectangle on your wall that matches the size of your sleeve.<ref>https://youtu.be/A_R71xYPyks?t=11</ref>",
                    "#Slide the sleeve into your wall caulk the sides to seal it. If you have a disassembled sleeve, put it together following the directions on the packaging. Slide the sleeve into the opening so the edges are flush against the inside of your wall. Then, apply caulk along the edges and in any holes or gaps and allow it to fully dry to create a tight seal.<ref>https://airfocused.com/air-cooling/wall/how-to-install-through-the-wall-air-conditioner-and-sleeve/</ref>",
                    "===How do you install an inwall air conditioner?===",
                    "#Put foam into the gap around the unit and install your frame. If there’s a gap between your air conditioning unit and the sleeve, it can affect how well your unit functions. Take some foam and stuff it into the space to help seal it. Then, put together the frame of your air conditioner and connect it to the front of the unit.<ref>https://youtu.be/OhoxHHoT57w?t=68</ref>"
